Z||000019||Nashco||05-07-2001||11:52 PM||||FYI, I'm putting an earlier (non-counter balanced, TBI) 4.3 in my VW...the later models with the counterbalance shaft don't have many compatible external parts with the early 4.3/3.8/350.  I am going for easy repairs on the trail, so I needed to be able to swap things quickly and cheaply.  Also, I am a firm believer in the fewer parts the better on the trail <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  But, most of this doesn't apply to our cars..hehe...well...most.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000020||fierogt88||05-08-2001||08:45 AM||||What about pistons/cams/heads?  What parts can the newer engines use?  I'm trying to get all the info I can, and it will eventually appear on a website for all to enjoy...<P>Jay, you must share your knowledge as well.  Don't make me send the fiero police.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000021||Nashco||05-09-2001||09:36 PM||||Pistons...they're the same as the 350 pistons.  You can get about any piston you could imagine for them, from stock low compression cast to forged high compression race pistons.  That's one of the best things about the 4.3, IMO.  Cams...depends on the year.  The pre-counter-balance-shaft motors all share similar cams, I believe.  There are a lot of aftermarket cams available, from stock to race cams (the 4.3 was used in stock car racing for a couple years).  Heads...that's a can of worms.  There are different heads for the vortec and non vortec heads.  There are some aluminum heads out there, Brodix were the most prevalent, but they cost a pretty penny.  As I said earlier, they used these motors in (Busch?) stock car racing for a few years...so if you really want you can go all out with them, the parts are available.  The intakes vary between throttle body, vortech, and aftermarket carburetor ones.  I don't believe there's much of anything aftermarket for the vortecs as far as intake/fuel delivery goes.  The vortec is a funky setup because it has a CPI (Central Port Injector, I believe) that is an electronic injector.  There are six hoses attached to this injector that feed to six poppet valves, that pop open with the correct amount of pressure on the line and vacuum from inside the cylinder.  This setup provides a big gain in torque, and some in hp, but is very temperamental.  It is reliable, just that if you ever have less than spec fuel pressure (48 lbs or so?) the engine just won't run.  The throttle bodies aren't as efficient, but still a big improvement over carbs because of computer controls and no problems with fuel slosh in racing/off camber situations.  Of course, we all know about carburetors, so no need to go there.  The only real question in doing an electronic setup is the v.s.s.  I'm not sure if our speedo sensors do the same thing as a v.s.s. (Vehicle Speed Sensor) for a GM truck, so that'd have to be researched a fair bit (or just testing it out) to see what would work.<P>About the engines, they came in more than just S10/Blazer and astro van stuff...most of the good motors from those vehicles get gobbled up quickly.  A semi-little-known fact is that these came in full size 1500 trucks with a 5 speed.  I have two engines from Astros (one has a bad head, one has a bad bottom end, planning to make a good one out of two), and I got an ENTIRE wiring harness from an 89 C1500 for engine, tail lights, fuel sender...everything.  I only chose the 89 because that's what the local yard had, and I coincidentally have an 89 throttle body (they're the same, but it's nice to know it HAS to match <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  One really cool thing some people never knew is that some Astro's (don't know which years, earlier I believe) came with tube manifolds.  Just like our 2.8 manifolds, these tube manifolds work great if a little attention is paid to them internally.  Since they came on the Astros, which have a cramped engine compartment, they are pretty good on space consumption and have GM reliability.<P>I didn't research a whole lot of stuff on the newer counterbalanced motors because I already had two older ones, I wanted less parts to break/deal with if something broke, vibration isn't an issue, I wanted the performance of injection setup on very steep inclines and hard driving on the whoopdees, and I wanted the ability to keep driving down the road if I dropped a few pounds of fuel pressure lower than what's required (A clogged fuel filter could easily bring the vortec to its knees).  This is mostly offroad requirements, but can still apply to a Fiero.  For example, if you're planning on Autocrossing...a carb setup won't cut it unless it is a very high dollar carb with center jets and anti-slosh features.  Also, some of us just don't want to fool with extra parts when working on our own cars, so it's worth a little vibration to have fewer things to deal with later.  Lastly, the older motors are obviously cheaper.  It might be recommended you use an older motor to test out your turbo setup...because it's cheaper to blow up a stock old motor than a mildly modified newer motor just because of a simple unforseen design glitch.  After you get everything figured out with motor mounts, cooling, brackets, pulleys, and exhaust pipes, you could then install the newer motor and *almost* everything would be the same for your high dollar motor that you no longer have to worry about grenading.<P>Phew...ok...I think I'll leave it at that for now.  If more people generate discussion about this, I could offer more, and I would also love to see others' thoughts and knowledge, because I am far from knowing everything!<P>Jay, I am really interested to hear from you about your experiences swapping into the Fiero.  What kind of electrical things did you deal with?  Have you considered polishing the intake?  They look awesome polished or powdercoated.  How'd you do your adapter and mounts?  All fabricated, modified v8 stuff, etc?  What're you using for the throttle cable, is it stock?  I don't see a cruise control unit, have you considered it?  I believe the stalk in our Fieros will work with the other units.  Heck, we might be able to adapt our whole system into it.  What's up with the starter relay solenoid?  What're you using for a starter?  What're you running for exhaust?  Thanks!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
